> how to set neovim as manpager?

✦ To set Neovim as your MANPAGER, you need to set the MANPAGER environment variable in your shell's configuration file (e.g., ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, or ~/.config/fish/config.fish).

  Here are a couple of options.

  Option 1: Simple Command

  You can set MANPAGER to a command that opens a new Neovim instance in read-only mode with the correct file type.

  For Bash or Zsh, add this to your ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc:

   1 export MANPAGER='nvim -c "set ft=man" -'

  For Fish, add this to your ~/.config/fish/config.fish:

   1 set -x MANPAGER 'nvim -c "set ft=man" -'

  Option 2: Using a Script

  For a more robust solution that handles nested man pages and other edge cases, you can use a script.

   1. Create the script:

      Create a file named nvman (or any other name you prefer) in a directory that's in your PATH (e.g., ~/.local/bin/).

   1     mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
   2     touch ~/.local/bin/nvman
   3     chmod +x ~/.local/bin/nvman

   2. Add the following content to the `~/.local/bin/nvman` file:

   1     #!/bin/sh
   2     nvim -c "set ft=man" -

   3. Set your `MANPAGER` to point to this script.

      For Bash or Zsh, in your ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc:

   1     export MANPAGER="~/.local/bin/nvman"

      For Fish, in your ~/.config/fish/config.fish:

   1     set -x MANPAGER ~/.local/bin/nvman

  After applying one of these methods, you'll need to either restart your shell or source your configuration file (e.g., source ~/.bashrc) for the changes to take effect. Then, when
  you run man <command>, it will open in Neovim.

> the color codes are messed up

✦ You are right. That happens because man outputs formatting control characters (like backspaces for bolding and underlining) that Neovim displays literally instead of interpreting
  them.

  The fix is to pipe the man page through col -b to remove these control characters before it reaches Neovim.

  If you used the script-based approach from my previous answer, you just need to modify your ~/.local/bin/nvman script.

  Update the `~/.local/bin/nvman` file to be:

   1 #!/bin/sh
   2 col -b | nvim -c "set ft=man" -

  This pipes the output of man through col -b, which cleans up the formatting, and then pipes the clean text into Neovim. Your MANPAGER environment variable setting can remain the
  same.


